## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Create a deterministic lifecycle and database-truth qualification harness that repeatedly drives
synthetic Tool Shed work through the same routes an operator uses: create an Idea, develop and
promote it into planning, create and work actionable descendants, close them in different orders,
reconcile outcomes, checkpoint and rebuild state, report it to the development dashboard, and
verify that every resulting status is true.

The harness should be deliberately low-reasoning and database-heavy. A model may provide small,
bounded fixture text where a route requires prose, but it does not decide whether the system is
correct. Exact machine-checkable invariants over stored identities, lineage, closure, evidence,
reporter delivery, hosted records, and rendered views form the test oracle.

This is broader than `IDEA-0019`. That Idea defines the recursive closure and lineage substrate;
this Idea qualifies Tool Shed as a complete operating system across real lifecycle routes and all
three development lanes. It should reveal gaps between individually passing components and the
truth an operator actually sees.

### Why It Matters

The development dashboard recently displayed two synthetic seed projects as if they were real
active projects alongside the Linux and Windows test beds. The individual services were healthy,
yet the operator-facing inventory was materially false. That is direct evidence that component
tests and isolated closure tests are not enough.

Tool Shed needs a repeatable proof that one source event does not become contradictory facts in
different stores or views. A lifecycle can look clean locally while its reporter is delayed, its
hosted projection is duplicated or stale, or the UI labels it incorrectly. Conversely, a display
problem must not be allowed to rewrite authoritative local state.

The harness should make those gaps cheap to reproduce and diagnose before development code is
promoted to a production release.

### Desired Outcome

A maintainer can run a bounded deterministic qualification suite against disposable web, Linux,
and Windows development fixtures and receive an exact pass/fail report with a retained replay
recipe for every failure. Given the same release candidate, fixture baseline, scenario seed, and
inputs, the run produces the same logical sequence and expected state.

For every checkpoint in a scenario, the harness compares three truth layers:

1. **Local authority:** canonical Tool Shed documents, cycles, requirements, lineage, evidence,
   closure rollups, checkpoints, and recovery findings in the fixture database.
2. **Transport and hosted projection:** reporter payload/outbox state, delivery identity and
   sequence, ingestion result, and the hosted development database rows derived from that report.
3. **Operator presentation:** dashboard inventory, counts, labels, filters, attention state, detail
   pages, provenance, and freshness as rendered for the operator.

The suite passes only when all applicable layers agree with the authoritative contract and every
expected exception is represented explicitly rather than disappearing or being guessed.

### Qualification Invariants

- Every created cycle and obligation has a stable, resolvable identity and valid immediate lineage.
- A parent remains effectively open while any governing child or deeper descendant is open,
  missing, unknown, invalid, stale, or unreconciled.
- Manual closure closes only the selected element; it never silently closes descendants.
- Closed-loop status requires current evidence for the exact obligation and subject revision.
- Terminal child outcomes propagate and reconcile exactly once to each governing parent
  obligation, including shared-child paths.
- A clean graph has zero unresolved orphans, cycles, conflicting lineage claims, missing governing
  requirements, stale rollups, or unpropagated outcomes.
- Full recursive evaluation and incremental rollups return the same semantic result at the same
  graph revision.
- Checkpoint, restart, rebuild, update, and replay preserve semantic state and do not resurrect
  retired work.
- Retried commands, duplicate reporter submissions, and repeated reconciliation are idempotent.
- Reporter delivery either drains to the expected hosted state or remains visibly pending/stale;
  transport failure never presents stale data as current.
- One stable project identity maps to one hosted project and one stable instance identity maps to
  one hosted instance. Reinstall, restart, alias, or hostname changes do not create phantom rows.
- Hosted counts, classifications, freshness, filters, detail pages, and hidden/retired behavior
  match the hosted database and never imply authority the server does not own.
- Each failure identifies the first divergent checkpoint, relevant IDs and revisions, expected and
  actual values, and a deterministic replay recipe without leaking secrets.

### Scenario Families

| Family | What it should vary and prove |
| --- | --- |
| Normal lifecycle | Idea through planning, campaign/work creation, evidence, reconciliation, and effective closure |
| Closure ordering | Parent/children closed in different orders; manual versus closed-loop; reopened or revised work |
| Graph shape | Deep chains, wide trees, shared children, nested obligations, missing parents, cycles, and reparenting |
| Interruption | Stop and resume between writes, evidence, rollup propagation, checkpoint, and reconciliation |
| Concurrency/idempotency | Duplicate commands, stale expected revisions, repeated delivery, and competing closure attempts |
| Projection recovery | Corrupt or remove rebuildable indexes, then compare deterministic rebuild with authority |
| Reporter disruption | Offline, delayed, reordered, duplicated, rejected, revoked, and reconnected delivery |
| Runtime persistence | Process restart, host reboot, client update, dashboard redeploy, and compatible schema migration |
| Operator truth | Phantom, duplicate, stale, hidden, retired, unknown, misclassified, and divergent project displays |
| Accumulation and scale | Many completed lifecycles plus active work, bounded reads, mutation cost, reporter backlog, and rebuild time |

Start with named hand-authored scenarios for the most important contracts. Add seeded deterministic
state-machine sequences to explore combinations and retain the seed plus a minimized failing action
sequence. Randomness without a stable seed or replay log does not qualify as evidence.

### First Acceptance Corpus

The recommended first corpus is nine named scenarios. It is intentionally small enough to diagnose
by hand but complete enough to cross the boundaries that have already produced misleading state.
Run the lifecycle scenarios on both Linux and Windows unless the scenario is explicitly a
platform-neutral hosted assertion.

| ID | Scenario | Required proof |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `QH-001` | Baseline identity and inventory | The development dashboard contains exactly the enrolled fixture projects and instances; optional seeds, aliases, and prior test rows do not appear as current projects |
| `QH-002` | Single complete lifecycle | Create one synthetic Idea, promote it through PRM into one governed work cycle, complete it with evidence, reconcile it, and prove every ancestor changes only at the correct checkpoint |
| `QH-003` | Branched closure ordering | Use two governing children and one non-governing child; mix manual and closed-loop closure and close them in reverse order while the parent remains open until every governing obligation is satisfied |
| `QH-004` | Deep and shared lineage | Exercise at least three descendant levels and one shared child; prove blocker propagation, obligation-scoped paths, and deduplicated rollup computation |
| `QH-005` | Revision invalidates proof | Close an obligation, revise its subject, prove prior evidence and rollup become stale, then re-prove the new revision without rewriting history |
| `QH-006` | Interrupted reconciliation and replay | Stop after child terminal state but before parent reconciliation, restart, replay the operation, and prove one terminal outcome, one reconciliation effect, and no active residue |
| `QH-007` | Reporter outage and convergence | Complete local work while delivery is unavailable, prove hosted state is visibly stale, reconnect, drain in order, and converge without duplicate project, instance, event, or work rows |
| `QH-008` | Checkpoint, rebuild, and presentation parity | Checkpoint and rebuild derived state, restart services, and prove recursive evaluation, indexed rollups, hosted counts, filters, details, and attention labels retain the same meaning |
| `QH-009` | Invalid graph fails closed | In an isolated database copy, inject a missing parent, conflicting lineage, and a cycle; prove each has a bounded recovery finding and blocks only its affected ancestry |

Every scenario has explicit checkpoints after material mutations. At each checkpoint, the oracle
compares a compact truth vector rather than loosely comparing logs:

```text
run and scenario identity
database, document, subject, graph, and reporter revisions
element IDs, immediate lineage, governing obligations, and blocker paths
local closure, evidence health, graph health, effective closure, and reason codes
outcome verdict, reconciliation state, and residual-work count
outbox depth, next sequence, delivery acknowledgement, and hosted freshness
hosted project/instance/work cardinality and rendered classifications
```

The corpus is a release-candidate gate only when every required platform/scenario pair passes.
Results are classified as `PASS`, `PRODUCT-FAIL`, `HARNESS-FAIL`, or `INFRA-BLOCKED`; infrastructure
and harness failures never masquerade as evidence that Tool Shed correctly rejected work.

### Fixture Isolation And Retention Contract

Normal lifecycle scenarios run append-only in the long-lived disposable Linux and Windows test
beds. Each run receives an explicit recorded serial and a deterministic namespace derived from the
candidate revision, corpus version, platform, scenario ID, serial, and seed. Reusing the same run
identity means resume or idempotent replay; a new serial creates a new retained qualification run.
Synthetic artifacts carry that run identity, but the fixtures keep their established project and
instance identities so the same reporter enrollment and hosted rows are exercised.

Normal success does not delete its artifacts. It closes and reconciles them, retains their
completed history, and proves a clean operational tail: zero run-owned active work, unresolved
lineage, stale rollups, unpropagated outcomes, pending outbox records, unexpected hosted rows, or
current UI entries. This accumulation is valuable test pressure and directly answers whether Tool
Shed remains clean as the database grows.

Do not restore or rewind the live fixture databases, reporter sequence, protected credentials, or
hosted ingestion cursor between normal scenarios. Rewinding only one side would manufacture stale
sequence and duplicate-identity behavior unrelated to the product contract.

Destructive graph corruption, interrupted-write injection, and migration damage run against an
ephemeral copy made from a recorded baseline. The controller must verify the exact disposable host,
workspace path, development environment sentinel, database digest, and stopped writer set before
mutation. The copy never shares the active reporter identity or credential. Initial destructive
tests may stop at local authority and rebuild proof; end-to-end destructive reporting is deferred
until the development server has a dedicated qualification namespace with exact run-bounded purge
semantics.

Routine harness execution has no broad cleanup operation. If an aborted normal run needs repair,
the repair command may affect only objects bearing its exact qualification run identity and must
preview IDs and counts before mutation. A full fixture reset remains an explicit maintainer
recovery operation outside ordinary qualification.

### Driver And Oracle Boundary

Use two related but distinct layers so deterministic database volume is not confused with model
behavior:

1. **Deterministic lifecycle driver:** apply fixed, versioned fixture payloads through the same
   guarded document, relationship, cycle, evidence, reconciliation, checkpoint, and reporting
   services used by normal commands. This supplies the high-volume repeatable corpus and must not
   call a model.
2. **Route smoke layer:** run a small bounded sample through the actual `ts:` interaction routes
   using low reasoning, then assert only the structural and database contract. Generated prose is
   not compared byte-for-byte and never becomes the pass/fail oracle for lifecycle truth.

Production writes use only public guarded mutations. Read-only SQL may independently inspect local
and hosted databases because using the same rollup/query implementation as both product and oracle
could certify a shared defect. Narrow internal hooks are allowed only for frozen time, controlled
process interruption, deterministic fault injection, and scratch-database inspection. Each hook
must be unavailable or fail closed outside the development qualification sentinel.

The hosted core uses database, view-model, and HTTP assertions for full coverage. A small real
browser layer is required for three operator-critical claims: project inventory/cardinality,
project detail closure/blocker state, and attention/freshness classification. Visual styling is not
the truth oracle, but displayed values and navigation targets are.

### Run Records And Replay Contract

Do not create a second operational database for the harness. Use four small versioned record types
with one-way references and content digests:

| Record | Ownership and purpose |
| --- | --- |
| Scenario specification | Checked-in, immutable test intent: ordered action kinds, required checkpoints, invariant IDs, supported platforms, and fixture payload references |
| Run manifest | Immutable generated input capsule binding one execution to the scenario digest, candidate commit and version, schema/evaluator/reporter versions, platform, exact project and instance IDs, explicit run serial, seed, baseline digests, clock mode, and target environment |
| Action journal | Append-only generated record of attempted actions, guarded input tokens, returned IDs/revisions, timestamps or logical ticks, stdout/stderr hashes, and interruption points |
| Result summary | Compact verdict and evidence index containing checkpoint outcomes, first divergence, timing distributions, retained artifact hashes, and replay command |

The scenario specification describes behavior, not database rows. The run manifest contains no
secrets and is sealed by its canonical JSON digest before the first mutation. The qualification
run ID is derived from that digest. Any retry with the same manifest is a resume/idempotency test;
changing candidate, target, fixture, serial, seed, or scenario creates a different run identity.

The action journal is evidence, never an input that silently changes intended behavior. Resume
reads the immutable manifest and journal, confirms the live target is at the last verified
checkpoint, then either continues the next action or reports divergence. It never guesses that a
partially observed action failed; it resolves the action's idempotency key against authoritative
state first.

Checked-in scenario specifications and compact release-candidate result summaries are versioned.
Raw journals, SQL extracts, browser captures, and logs remain under ignored generated evidence;
their hashes and relative locations appear in the summary when retained.

### Independent Truth-Vector Oracle

Each checkpoint declares a list of invariant IDs and selectors, not a copied expected database
snapshot. A selector is scoped by stable project/instance identity plus qualification run ID and
exact element or obligation IDs returned by prior actions. Assertions support exact value, set
equality, cardinality, absence, monotonic revision/sequence, digest equality, and bounded eventual
convergence.

The oracle produces one normalized truth vector per checkpoint:

```text
checkpoint identity and logical tick
authoritative element, obligation, lineage, evidence, and outcome facts
independently calculated ancestry, blockers, and effective closure
stored projection values and graph/evaluator revision bindings
outbox events, idempotency keys, sequence cursor, and delivery disposition
hosted project, instance, snapshot, work, and freshness facts
HTTP/view-model values and critical browser-visible values and links
unexpected-row sets for every selected scope
```

The reference closure evaluator reads canonical element envelopes, parent-owned requirements,
current closure/evidence records, assertions, and tombstones, then builds its own in-memory graph.
It must not import or query the product's ancestor, blocker, or rollup evaluator. It compares that
independent result with the indexed projection. Likewise, reporter checks compare immutable outbox
payload and sequence facts with hosted ingestion/base rows before checking hosted derived views;
dashboard checks derive expected labels from the public status contract rather than calling the
same view helper under test.

Every truth-vector field names its source layer and authority class: `authoritative`,
`transport-record`, `hosted-projection`, or `presentation`. Derived layers may agree with authority
but cannot promote themselves to it. Unknown or unavailable observations remain explicit and
prevent `PASS` for checkpoints that require them.

The oracle evaluates both presence and absence. Each scope must prove the expected rows exist and
that the unexpected-row set is empty, which directly catches phantom projects, duplicate
instances, resurrected work, stale blockers, and hidden active residue.

### Provisional Scale And Performance Budgets

Correctness is absolute; timing budgets apply only after semantic parity passes. Record complete
distributions and target details. Use absolute ceilings for deterministic local/server work and a
relative regression guard against the same target's accepted baseline. Network and browser timing
remain advisory unless they exceed a generous runaway ceiling.

Reuse the already-qualified `IDEA-0019` graph corpus and budgets rather than introducing a second
standard:

| Surface | Reference load | Initial ceiling |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Closure summary | 25,000 elements / 100,000 edges / depth 128 | p95 20 ms |
| First 100 blockers | Same graph | p95 50 ms |
| Mutation through up to 128 ancestors | Same graph | p95 250 ms |
| Independent full graph rebuild | Same graph | 60 seconds |
| Hosted closure API server time | Same logical status contract | p95 250 ms, excluding network |

Add lifecycle-system budgets at the first proof-of-concept scale:

| Surface | Reference load | Initial ceiling |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Ordinary guarded local lifecycle mutation | Database after 100 retained complete qualification lifecycles | p95 1 second, excluding declared proof commands and rebuilds |
| Local status/truth-vector extraction for one run | Same accumulated database | p95 1 second |
| Eligible reporter backlog drain | Up to 64 queued full-state reports through the existing bounded safety pass | 60 seconds and zero pending |
| Post-drain hosted convergence | Latest accepted sequence through hosted base rows, API, and view model | 15 seconds on the development LAN |
| Dashboard server rendering | 100 projects, 500 instances, and 10,000 current work rows | p95 500 ms per qualified list/detail request, excluding network |
| Critical browser smoke | Inventory, project detail, or attention page on the development LAN | 3-second runaway ceiling per navigation; record actual timing as advisory |
| Deterministic `QH-001`–`QH-009` core | One platform, excluding low-reasoning route smoke and deliberate retry holds | 15 minutes |

At each scale, p95 latency must also remain no worse than twice the accepted same-target baseline.
An absolute pass with a greater-than-twofold regression is `PRODUCT-FAIL` until explained and the
budget is explicitly revised. A target too slow or unstable to collect the required sample count
is `INFRA-BLOCKED`, not a timing pass.

The first accumulation step is 100 retained complete lifecycles per platform. After that passes,
expand without changing semantics to 1,000 retained lifecycles and at least 100,000 append-only
history/assertion/event records. The larger run initially measures and detects superlinear growth;
it does not gain a hard absolute release ceiling until Linux and Windows baselines exist. Database
file size, WAL size, checkpoint time, query plans, row counts by table, and retained evidence size
are recorded so cleanup pressure cannot be mistaken for semantic correctness.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Run destructive and corruption cases only in the explicitly disposable Linux, Windows, and web
  development fixtures. Production data and production deployment are outside this Idea's initial
  qualification boundary.
- Use public Tool Shed commands and normal reporting routes for the main acceptance path. Permit
  narrow internal hooks only where controlled fault injection or independent database inspection
  cannot be achieved through public behavior.
- Keep fixture prose minimal, deterministic, and structurally sufficient. Do not spend model
  reasoning on creative content that is irrelevant to database transitions.
- Inspect authoritative stores independently of the code path being tested so a shared defect does
  not make both the implementation and its oracle agree on the same wrong answer.
- Do not make elapsed wall-clock timing the semantic oracle. Use controllable clocks or bounded
  waits for freshness, leases, retries, and expiry where practical.
- Do not turn the harness into a second implementation of Tool Shed or a production cleanup tool.
- Initial success is a proof of concept on development fixtures, not authorization to release a
  candidate to production.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Public-command scenario driver | Exercises the operator contract and catches integration gaps | Slower and less direct fault control | Preferred main acceptance path |
| Direct library/API driver | Fast, precise, and easy to generate many transitions | Can bypass command routing and conceal integration defects | Useful for lower-level generated tests, not sufficient alone |
| Exact hand-authored corpus | Easy to understand and stable in release gates | Covers fewer combinations | Required foundation |
| Seeded state-machine sequences | Explores ordering and graph combinations at high database volume | Needs shrinking and strong diagnostics | Add after the foundation |
| Reset fixture for each scenario | Strong isolation and reproducibility | Misses long-term accumulation defects | Use for destructive and fault cases |
| Retain a shared synthetic history | Reveals buildup, stale-state, and performance defects | Harder failure isolation | Use as a separate accumulation suite |
| Database plus HTTP assertions | Fast and precise coverage of hosted truth | May miss browser rendering behavior | Required core web proof |
| Small browser smoke layer | Verifies operator-visible inventory and navigation | More environment-sensitive | Recommended for critical display invariants |

## Open Questions

- How should a failing generated sequence be minimized without removing the timing or ordering fact
  that caused the failure?
- Which evidence bundle is safe and sufficient to retain after a failed run, and what should be
  automatically discarded after a pass?
- What dedicated hosted qualification namespace and authenticated cleanup contract would safely
  allow end-to-end destructive server cases without making synthetic projects look operational?
- Which structural assertions are sufficient for the low-reasoning `ts:` route smoke, given that
  generated wording is intentionally non-deterministic?

## Decisions

- Treat this as a separate system-qualification Idea, with `IDEA-0019` supplying recursive closure
  semantics rather than absorbing the harness scope.
- Make the suite deterministic, replayable, database-heavy, and low-reasoning.
- Use exact invariant checks—not model judgment—as the pass/fail authority.
- Compare local authority, reporter/hosted projection, and operator presentation at explicit
  checkpoints.
- Exercise the real web, Linux, and Windows development lanes before any production qualification.
- Preserve both an isolated correctness suite and an accumulation/scale suite because they expose
  different failure classes.
- Fail closed on unknown, missing, stale, contradictory, or unreconciled state; never convert lack
  of proof into success.
- Adopt `QH-001` through `QH-009` as the first hand-authored acceptance corpus.
- Define normal cleanliness as a closed, reconciled, fully delivered operational tail rather than
  an empty database; retain completed synthetic history to expose accumulation defects.
- Never rewind live reporter sequence or hosted ingestion state during routine qualification.
- Restrict destructive fault injection to verified ephemeral database copies until an explicitly
  isolated hosted qualification namespace exists.
- Separate the no-model deterministic lifecycle driver from a small low-reasoning `ts:` route
  smoke layer.
- Use independent read-only database inspection as part of the oracle, with narrow development-only
  hooks for time, interruption, and injected corruption.
- Require real-browser assertions for inventory, project-detail closure, and attention/freshness;
  cover the rest through database, view-model, and HTTP checks.
- Use four content-bound records—scenario specification, immutable run manifest, append-only action
  journal, and compact result summary—without adding a harness database.
- Derive qualification run identity from the sealed run-manifest digest; the explicit serial is an
  input so replay stays deterministic without preventing intentional accumulation runs.
- Resume only from a verified checkpoint and resolve uncertain actions through their idempotency
  keys before continuing.
- Make checkpoint expectations invariant- and selector-based rather than copied database snapshots.
- Rebuild reference ancestry and effective closure independently from canonical inputs; do not
  import the product projection/evaluator into the oracle.
- Tag every truth-vector field with its source layer and authority class, and require both expected
  presence and zero unexpected rows.
- Reuse the `IDEA-0019` 25,000-element/100,000-edge/depth-128 graph budgets and add provisional
  lifecycle, reporter, hosted-render, browser-runaway, and 15-minute per-platform corpus ceilings.
- Start accumulation qualification at 100 complete retained lifecycles per platform, then measure
  1,000 lifecycles and 100,000 history records before freezing larger-scale absolute budgets.
- Fail a greater-than-twofold same-target performance regression even when it remains below the
  absolute ceiling, unless the budget is explicitly revised from evidence.

## Don't Forget

- The phantom seed projects are a concrete regression fixture, not merely background context.
- A healthy service response proves availability, not truthful inventory or lifecycle state.
- A drained reporter queue proves delivery, not correct ingestion or presentation.
- The harness must inspect absence: extra rows, resurrected elements, hidden blockers, stale
  assertions, and duplicate identities are failures even when all expected rows also exist.
- Parent/child proof must cover grandchildren and shared descendants, not only one-level examples.
- Tests should state which layer owns each fact so a derived view cannot accidentally become the
  recovery authority.
- Timeouts and infrastructure errors are `unknown` or harness failures, not evidence that the
  product correctly rejected a lifecycle transition.
- Every retained replay case must name the candidate revision, fixture baseline, platform, scenario
  version, seed, and action sequence.
- A qualification run identifier must tag artifacts and evidence, not replace the stable project
  or reporter-instance identity under test.
- Cleanup must never be the mechanism that makes a normal run look closed; closure, reconciliation,
  and delivery are what make it clean.
- The oracle must be able to detect unexpected extra rows as well as missing expected rows.

